        "content": "Primary health care means accessible first-contact care combined with prevention and continuing support for population health. In Iran, its best-known community worker is the **behvarz**, whose work forms part of the rural health-house network. WHO’s historical case-study overview identifies the development, training, and supervision of these workers as important to the expansion of health services, particularly for rural communities. It associates the network’s development with improvements in maternal and child health and life expectancy. These are historical assessments, rather than a guarantee of identical services in every locality today. [s2]\n\nThe network takes different forms across settlements. Rural communities are served through health houses and rural comprehensive health centers; urban services include health posts and urban comprehensive health centers. The distinction matters when interpreting references to a local “health center”: the term does not necessarily describe the same facility, staffing arrangement, or role everywhere. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "system-relationships",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "The public primary-care management framework",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  M[\"Ministry of Health and Medical Education\"] -->|\"National policy and planning\"| U[\"State universities of medical sciences\"]\n  U -->|\"Regional planning and oversight\"| N[\"County health networks\"]\n  N -->|\"Organize rural services\"| R[\"Rural health centers and health houses\"]\n  N -->|\"Organize urban services\"| C[\"Urban health centers and health posts\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The arrows describe policy, oversight, and service organization in the framework reported by the cited study. They do not represent patient referral instructions, funding flows, or a complete map of all Iranian health providers.",
        "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "financing-reform",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Access also depends on financing",
        "content": "An extensive service network does not by itself settle who pays for treatment or how well households are protected from its cost. WHO’s 2020 financing and governance assessment describes reform efforts addressing insurance coverage, patient cost-sharing, benefit packages, and the purchasing of services. Purchasing here means deciding which services to pay for, from which providers, and under what arrangements. [s3]\n\nThe assessment places the Health Transformation Plan within this broader reform history. Its scope shows why health-system performance cannot be judged from hospital construction or insurance enrollment alone: service availability, financial protection, purchasing arrangements, and governance all require attention. The report presents policy options for further progress toward universal health coverage; its discussion should not be read as confirmation that every reform objective was achieved or remains implemented in the same form. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "implementation-and-resilience",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Formal structure and practical performance",
        "content": "Evidence from practitioners and researchers complicates a purely organizational account. A qualitative study published in 2022 identified problems involving governance, personnel, resources, financial management, service delivery, and collaboration beyond the health sector. Its interviews provide evidence about perceived implementation difficulties, not national estimates of how frequently each problem occurs. [s1]\n\nThe COVID-19 response also demonstrated how community services could support emergency care. WHO described primary-care teams using telephone follow-up, community records, and triage to help identify suspected cases and direct people toward further assessment. These were documented pandemic-response activities, rather than evidence that every digital tool or procedure continued unchanged afterward. [s4]\n\nThe central distinction is between having a connected institutional framework and making its connections work consistently. Iran’s system is best understood by examining community services, regional management, and national financing together, while keeping historical achievements separate from present-day measures of access and quality. [s1][s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
